WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 19: The Great Diamond Hoax of 1872: When Tycoons Bought a Seeded Mountain

1
00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:04.960
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:06.160
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:12.560
[Rebecca] In the gilded aftermath of the California Gold Rush, San Francisco was the epicenter of speculative fever.

4
00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:21.280
[Rebecca] Millionaires were made overnight in silver and gold, and the Titans of American finance believed that anything was possible in the untamed American West.

5
00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:31.840
[Rebecca] In 1872, two dusty, unassuming prospectors exploited that greed to pull off the greatest financial swindle in American history, the Great Diamond Hoax.

6
00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:50.880
[Rebecca] Armed with $35,000 worth of industrial diamond, refused purchased from gem merchants in London, the two grifters salted a remote mesa in the Rocky Mountains, fooling world-renowned jeweler Charles Tiffany, the powerful Bank of California, and International Tycoons into buying millions of dollars in shares.

7
00:00:50.880 --> 00:01:01.120
[Rebecca] What unraveled the scheme was not an auditor or an undercover detective, but a brilliant young government geologist who noticed something peculiar about how diamonds grow.

8
00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:04.240
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story.

9
00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:12.720
[George] In late 1871, two weather-beaten, bearded prospectors walked into the San Francisco headquarters of the Bank of California.

10
00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:15.840
[George] Their names were Philip Arnold and John Slack.

11
00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:24.800
[George] They were cousins from Kentucky who had spent decades prospecting in the gold fields of California and the silver mines of Nevada without striking it rich.

12
00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:34.800
[George] Wearing mud-stained buckskin jackets and battered hats, they approached the teller window and asked to place a heavy leather pouch into the bank's secure vault for safe keeping.

13
00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:42.640
[George] When asked what the pouch contained, the prospectors acted nervous and evasive, whispering that they did not wish to draw attention.

14
00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:53.200
[George] Word of the mysterious deposit quickly reached William Chapman Ralston, the flamboyant founder of the Bank of California and the undisputed king of West Coast Finance.

15
00:01:53.200 --> 00:02:03.160
[George] Ralston had made a staggering fortune financing the legendary Comstock load in Virginia City, and he was perpetually hunting for the next colossal resource discovery.

16
00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:07.240
[George] Ralston summoned Arnold and Slack to his private executive office.

17
00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:21.080
[George] With apparent reluctance, Arnold untied the leather thongs and dumped the contents onto Ralston's mahogany desk, a glittering cascade of uncut, raw diamonds, deep red rubies, green emeralds and sapphires.

18
00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:29.960
[George] The prospectors claimed they had stumbled upon an uncharted desert mesa in the Rocky Mountains where precious gems lay scattered on the surface like gravel.

19
00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:42.120
[George] Ralston was known as the man who built San Francisco, having financed the Palace Hotel, the Spring Valley Water Company, and the Transcontinental Steamship lines with effortless audacity.

20
00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:47.320
[George] In reality, Philip Arnold and John Slack had discovered no diamond field.

21
00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:52.600
[George] They had orchestrated one of the most meticulously planned confidence games in human history.

22
00:02:52.600 --> 00:03:05.160
[George] Months earlier, Arnold had used $5,000 in personal savings to purchase a collection of low-grade, uncut diamonds and rough gemstones from Native American traders and dealers in Arizona.

23
00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:15.240
[George] Using these initial stones as collateral, the conspirators secured loans, and Arnold traveled secretly to London, the world capital of the diamond trade.

24
00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:27.080
[George] In the shadowed gem markets of Hatton Garden, Arnold bought $35,000 worth of low grade, industrial rough diamonds, along with uncut rubies and sapphires from Ceylon.

25
00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:36.760
[George] These stones were known in the trade as bort, flawed, discolored stones discarded by jewelry cutters and used primarily for diamond-tipped drill bits.

26
00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:48.080
[George] Returning to the United States, Arnold and Slack traveled on horseback into the remote wilderness along the Wyoming Colorado border, near a rugged sandstone landmark called Table Rock.

27
00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:53.040
[George] Over several freezing weeks, the two men painstakingly salted the landscape.

28
00:03:53.040 --> 00:04:08.880
[George] They used bone-nitting needles and iron rods to punch small holes into anthills, sandstone crevices and cedar roots, dropping uncut diamonds and rubies into the dirt and smoothing over the surface so they appeared naturally embedded by geological forces.

29
00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:21.200
[George] Arnold was a shrewd, patient schemer who had studied mineralogy books and gem catalogues, knowing that uncut industrial stones bought in Europe could easily fool even experienced American mining men.

30
00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:27.440
[George] William Ralston was completely hooked by the prospect of controlling the world's greatest diamond monopoly.

31
00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:36.160
[George] South Africa's Kimberley Diamond mines had just been discovered in 1867, turning European mining syndicates into global powerhouses.

32
00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:40.560
[George] Ralston envisioned an American gem empire that would eclipse Africa.

33
00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:49.920
[George] To verify the authenticity of the stones, Ralston assembled a syndicate of the most prominent men in America, including former Union General George B.

34
00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:55.760
[George] McClellan, prominent mining financier William Lent, and Senator William Stewart.

35
00:04:55.760 --> 00:05:05.920
[George] Ralston took a sample bag of the gems to New York City to be appraised by Charles Tiffany, the founder of the world's most prestigious jewelry firm, Tiffany and Company.

36
00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:13.360
[George] Tiffany examined the rough stones under magnifying loops in his Manhattan showroom, completely blinded by the excitement.

37
00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:21.440
[George] He issued a formal, breathless appraisal declaring that the sample was of the finest water and worth at least $150,000.

38
00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:26.240
[George] Tiffany stated that the discovery was authentic and would yield millions.

39
00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:43.080
[George] With Tiffany's prestigious endorsement, the syndicate formed the San Francisco and New York Mining and Commercial Company, capitalized at $10 million, with shares quickly purchased by elite European and American financiers, including Baron Nathaniel de Rothschild.

40
00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:54.920
[George] Charles Tiffany had spent 30 years building the most celebrated jewelry empire in the Western Hemisphere, making his enthusiastic appraisal the ultimate golden seal of legitimacy for investors.

41
00:05:54.920 --> 00:06:04.680
[George] Before handing over vast sums of cash, the syndicate insisted on sending an independent mining expert to inspect the actual diamond field in the wilderness.

42
00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:14.280
[George] They hired Henry Janin, one of the most respected and highly paid mining consulting engineers in the United States, who had never failed in assessing a mine.

43
00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:21.480
[George] Philip Arnold agreed to the inspection, but demanded absolute secrecy to prevent claim jumpers from learning the location.

44
00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:35.400
[George] Arnold met Janin and Ralston's representatives at a remote train depot in Wyoming, blindfolded them, and led them on a dizzying, circuitous four-day horseback ride through canyons and gulches to disorient their sense of geography.

45
00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:40.920
[George] When they finally arrived at the sandstone mesa, Janin began digging into the anthills.

46
00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:48.520
[George] Within minutes, Janin and the investors were pulling gleaming diamonds and rubies directly out of the sandy soil with their bare hands.

47
00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:50.760
[George] Janin was utterly astonished.

48
00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:57.320
[George] In his official technical report, the veteran engineer declared that the property was beyond all calculation.

49
00:06:57.320 --> 00:07:03.720
[George] With twenty laborers, the company could extract $1 million worth of diamonds a month from the mesa.

50
00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:09.800
[George] Janin was so convinced that he immediately purchased $1,000 shares of the company's stock himself.

51
00:07:09.800 --> 00:07:26.000
[George] Satisfied that the discovery was the greatest bonanza in American history, Ralston's syndicate paid Philip Arnold and John Slack $660,000 in cash, equivalent to nearly $20 million today, to buy out their majority ownership in the claims.

52
00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:38.080
[George] Janon was accompanied by prominent mining figures and Wall Street representatives, all of whom became so intoxicated by finding raw diamonds in anthills that they abandoned standard scientific testing.

53
00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:46.800
[George] While the Tycoons toasted their fortune with champagne in San Francisco and New York, a young government scientist smelled something impossible.

54
00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:55.440
[George] His name was Clarence King, the brilliant 30-year-old director of the United States Geological Exploration of the 40th parallel.

55
00:07:55.440 --> 00:08:03.680
[George] King was one of the foremost geological mines in the world, having spent five years mapping the exact terrain between California and Wyoming.

56
00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:18.080
[George] King knew that the geological conditions required to form diamonds, immense volcanic heat and crushing mantle pressure, were completely incompatible with the formation of rubies and sapphires, which grow in entirely different metamorphic rock.

57
00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:24.960
[George] To find all three gems coexisting naturally on a sandstone desert plateau was a physical impossibility.

58
00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:37.040
[George] Using subtle clues from Henry Jannon's travel notes, King and two assistants traced the route, locating the salted mesa near Table Rock in northwest Colorado in November 1872.

59
00:08:37.040 --> 00:08:43.520
[George] King spent hours on his knees examining the dirt under a field microscope, and what he found was damning.

60
00:08:43.520 --> 00:08:48.240
[George] Diamonds were found only in loose topsoil, never in the bedrock below.

61
00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:53.040
[George] Anthills contained diamonds only on the surface where a human hand had poked a hole.

62
00:08:53.040 --> 00:09:01.320
[George] And most damning of all, King discovered a cut diamond that bore the unmistakable scratch marks of a London Lapidary's grinding wheel.

63
00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:11.800
[George] Clarence King was a graduate of Yale's Sheffield Scientific School, combining rigorous laboratory mineralogy with years of rugged field exploration in the Great Basin.

64
00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:14.120
[George] Clarence King did not hesitate.

65
00:09:14.120 --> 00:09:24.280
[George] He boarded the Transcontinental Railroad and rushed to San Francisco, walking directly into William Ralston's office on November 25, 1872.

66
00:09:24.280 --> 00:09:31.400
[George] King slammed his geological report on Ralston's desk with a blunt declaration The Diamond Field is a fraud, Mr.

67
00:09:31.400 --> 00:09:32.360
[George] Ralston.

68
00:09:32.360 --> 00:09:34.280
[George] The mountain has been salted.

69
00:09:34.280 --> 00:09:36.280
[George] Ralston was devastated.

70
00:09:36.280 --> 00:09:51.000
[George] To protect the public and preserve his honor, the Bank of California immediately published King's findings in full on the front page of the newspapers and refunded every dollar invested by outside shareholders, absorbing the colossal financial loss.

71
00:09:51.000 --> 00:10:00.280
[George] The Great Diamond hoax made Clarence King a national hero, cementing his appointment as the very first director of the United States Geological Survey.

72
00:10:00.280 --> 00:10:04.440
[George] The two swindlers, meanwhile, had executed a clean escape.

73
00:10:04.440 --> 00:10:09.960
[George] John Slack vanished into the Arizona desert, living under an alias until his death.

74
00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:19.320
[George] Philip Arnold returned home to Kentucky a wealthy man, using his hundreds of thousands of dollars to buy a magnificent livestock estate and establish a bank.

75
00:10:19.320 --> 00:10:29.240
[George] When mining investors sued him in court, Arnold calmly denied knowing the field was salted, claiming he was merely a simple prospector who had been deceived himself.

76
00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:37.800
[George] He settled the lawsuit by returning a modest fraction of the money, keeping the vast majority of his fortune until his death in 1878.

77
00:10:37.800 --> 00:10:51.600
[George] The Great Diamond Hoax stands as America's greatest financial fable, an enduring reminder that when greed blinds the smartest men in the room, even a salted mountain of British industrial refuse can look like a diamond mine.

78
00:10:51.600 --> 00:11:04.560
[George] The scandal completely reshaped American mining finance, leading to the establishment of rigorous, independent geological standards and the professionalization of the United States Geological Survey.

79
00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:12.160
[Rebecca] The Great Diamond Hoax of 1872 remains the undisputed masterpiece of American financial confidence tricks.

80
00:11:12.160 --> 00:11:21.200
[Rebecca] It proves that no matter how sophisticated the bankers or how prestigious the experts, human greed will always be the easiest commodity on Earth to exploit.

81
00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:24.240
[Rebecca] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

82
00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:25.440
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

83
00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:28.720
[Rebecca] Join us next time for another forgotten chapter of history.

84
00:11:28.720 --> 00:11:31.120
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

